WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
When you leave the International Airport
of Rio de Janeiro and head towards the

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.000
south of the city which is where all the
beaches are,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.400
you pass a sprawling informal settlement called Maré.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.820
it's one of hundreds of neglected shanty
towns like this in Rio.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.240
It goes on for miles. But when you pass
by there today

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.960
all you see is this wall.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.820
Look at this map of Rio:

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:44.100
Here's the part that you probably know.
It's the South Zone it's where all the

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.110
iconic beaches are. Maré is in Rio's
North Zone which is where most of the

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:52.559
city's poor live.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.980
They don't have sewage systems they
don't have housing rights they don't

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.640
have anything, but you know the city is
really concerned about how loud the cars

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.970
are because they're worried about you
know the ears of the poor people

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.030
that don't have food in their stomachs.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:26.900
The city just install the big new schoolin this community a few months ago.

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:29.940
You'll note that when we get to this
part of the highway,

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.460
the wall becomes totally transparent,
giving us a perfect view of the shiny new school

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.950
Every time international attention comes to Rio, the
city scrambles to build up

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.030
infrastructure around tourism for
visitors to see that it's this amazing city

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.659
The problem is the visitors will come
and they go,

00:01:50.659 --> 00:01:53.359
but the people of Rio are here to stay,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.640
and they're frustrated that their governments spends
so much money to build up certain parts

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.340
of the city and completely neglects
others.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.479
The Olympics is no different in this
case. In fact it's probably the biggest

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:09.470
excuse Rio has to pour tons of money
into making the city look good.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.239
This is Patricia. She rides the buses
here in Rio and has noticed a major

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:16.459
change in the bus routes recently.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:31.940
Patricia is showing me a few examples of the 11 bus
lines that were cut between the poor

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.319
North zone and the touristy rich South
Zone, all in preparation for the Olympics.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:39.920
It's now much harder for a resident of
the North Zone to get down to the

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.350
beaches of Ipanema or Copacabana.

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.810
So why put the bus lines?

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.240
If discriminatory bus lines are bad here's where it gets worse.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.800
So back to this
map: out here in the west is a place

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.750
called Barra (Baha) R's are pronounced like H
in Portuguese. This is a new part of the

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:17.230
city where a lot of the Olympics action
is happening. It's where the Olympic

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.700
park is going to be built. And because of
this it's home to what one real estate

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:26.590
publication is calling a "cosmopolitan
awakening." Tons of real estate investment.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:30.130
And of course a bunch of dramatic
promotional videos to go with it.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:42.020
there's this one guy in Carlos Carvalho. He's
a real estate developer and owns

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.850
64 million square feet in Barra. Last year
in a series of interviews with big

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.720
publications, Carvalho sketched out his
dream for Barra.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:57.200
His goal is to turn this place into a
"new Rio" a city for the "elite ... of good taste

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.230
noble housing not housing for the poor."

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:04.310
This guy's that 12 richest person in
Brazil and he's got a ton of political

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.810
influence to make his dream happen. Here
he is with Rios mayor who's reelection

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:13.250
campaign he generously donated to. They're
just, you know, looking over plans for how

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:14.900
they're going to reshape Barra.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:20.000
But there's one big problem for people
like Carlos carvalho and his dream to

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.760
make Barra a haven for the rich. If you're
interested in land value, the less poor people you

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:26.840
who have in your land

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.860
the greater value can give to it.  They
think of the city as a place for you to

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.840
invest and not a place to live in.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.220
Over the years little settlements of a
few hundred families have popped up in Barra.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.640
It's usually workers unable to find
affordable housing and creating

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.330
communities of their own. These places
have been around for decades and many of

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:48.470
them have gained legal status for their
property. But to the luxury-minded

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.720
developers of this new part of town,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.740
these informal settlements represent a
barrier to their plan.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:58.160
So when the Olympic park was planned for
this area of Rio

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.430
it wasn't much of a surprise when the
city came in with eminent domain

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.300
eviction orders, telling these
communities that they would be moved to

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.650
public housing complexes usually far out
of sight of any international visitors.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:13.970
Most communities left, some happily
taking the money that the city gave them,

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.560
some mounted intense but failed
resistance. But I visited one community

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.040
that didn't give up on the fight to keep
their homes.

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:45.499
Vila Autódromo was a community of around 600
families near where the olympic park is being built.

00:05:45.499 --> 00:05:49.849
it's not on the actual park property but
it's in the sight of the park.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:53.920
This is what it looks like today.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:57.860
People
chose to go there because there was no

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.669
drug trafficking or militia. It was very
safe very-- a good sense of community.

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:03.319
close to jobs and schools.

00:06:03.319 --> 00:06:06.979
It's hard to know that when you just
visit the community, but you kind of get

00:06:06.979 --> 00:06:10.999
a sense of that by seeing the people
who are still resisting, because

00:06:10.999 --> 00:06:15.319
they're holding on to that memory and
they want to keep some of that alive.

00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:18.139
After years of fighting with the city
hall,

00:06:18.139 --> 00:06:23.059
only 20 families of the original six
hundred remain in this community.

00:06:23.059 --> 00:06:27.529
Fierce protesting in a flurry of international
press got the mayor to finally concede,

00:06:27.529 --> 00:06:31.639
saying that the twenty families could
stay, on condition that the city would

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:34.459
build them nicer-looking homes, lest,

00:06:34.459 --> 00:06:37.789
heaven forbid, the international
community catch a glimpse of the real Rio.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:28.520
Through the long fight, some of Vila Autódromo was able to stay.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.219
But this is rare. Most
communities that receive eviction orders

00:07:32.219 --> 00:07:36.120
no longer exists. At least seventy seven
thousand two hundred people have been

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.719
removed from their homes in Rio de
Janeiro since 2009.

00:07:39.719 --> 00:07:42.870
That's according to government data. And
much of this to make way for

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:47.969
infrastructure and real estate projects
associated with the World Cup and the Olympic Games.

00:07:47.969 --> 00:07:51.090
So it's kind of a shame because the
Olympics end up coming in and kind of

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:54.509
whitewashing areas

00:07:54.509 --> 00:07:59.819
and reframing them  you lose a
lot of the personality of the city.

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:02.819
of course there have been numerous benefits
to the people of Rio

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:07.699
thanks to the Olympics investments: New
bus lines, revitalization of all parts of the city,

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:12.810
museums, parks. This stuff will make life
better in the city for sure. But in the end

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:16.620
Billions of public dollars that were
supposed to benefit the people ended up

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.900
bowing to the interests of a few people
with a lot of money.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:25.400
And instead of investing in the underserved Rio will once again hide them from view.

